<header><p class="st-kicker">county · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">County of Oettingen</h1><p class="st-meta">1147 CE – 1522 CE (approx.) · capital Oettingen in Bayern</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Swabian county in the Ries crater whose counts built a territory in the Nördlinger Ries before splitting into Protestant and Catholic lines.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Count Ludwig of Oettingen appears around 1147, holding lands in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nordlinger-ries">Ries</a>, a fertile meteorite crater basin in Swabia. The counts were relatives of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hohenstaufen">Staufer</a> and expanded their territory through purchases and offices, making it one of the larger counties in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swabia">Swabia</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The counts ruled villages and small towns around Oettingen, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wallerstein">Wallerstein</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harburg-swabia">Harburg</a>, but the imperial city of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-nordlingen">Nördlingen</a> in the middle of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nordlinger-ries">Ries</a> stayed outside their control. The family later divided into several lines, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> split them by confession.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1522 the county was divided between <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-oettingen-spielberg">Oettingen-Oettingen</a>, which turned Protestant, and Catholic <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-oettingen-wallerstein">Oettingen-Wallerstein</a>. Later principalities, they were mediatised in 1806 and became part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>. The princes of Oettingen-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wallerstein">Wallerstein</a> still live at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harburg-swabia">Harburg</a> and Wallerstein.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/county-of-oettingen.jpg" alt="County of Oettingen" width="320" height="302"><figcaption>Image: M. F. Cnopf - Homann Heirs · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Oettingen_Counties_1744.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1147 CE – 1522 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>county</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Oettingen in Bayern</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ludwig of Oettingen</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Swabian German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Oettingen</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1334&amp;focus=county-of-oettingen" aria-label="County of Oettingen on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4925 469 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where County of Oettingen lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5245 725L5229 694M5245 725L5242 727M5245 725L5245 725"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5242 727h0M5245 725h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5245 725h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5245 725h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1334&amp;focus=county-of-oettingen">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
